    "result_text": "Green. Here's what I implemented:\n\n## Summary\n\nThe requirement: expose `telephoneDisplay` \u2014 the stored E.164 telephone formatted for humans (country code, space, national digits grouped in threes, e.g. `+61 412 345 678`) \u2014 while keeping `telephone` as raw E.164.\n\nTwo changes:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added a new read-only `telephoneDisplay` string property to the `Owner` schema. Since DTOs are generated by the openapi-generator plugin, this produces the `getTelephoneDisplay()`/`setTelephoneDisplay()` accessors on `OwnerDto`.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Added a `@Mapping(target = \"telephoneDisplay\", ...)` and a `telephoneDisplay(Owner)` default method. It:\n   - Returns the stored value unchanged when it's `null` or not a `+`-prefixed all-digits E.164 string.\n   - Splits off the country calling code (matching known codes `61`/`1` longest-first, consistent with the controller's `NATIONAL_NUMBER_LENGTHS`; falls back to a single-digit code otherwise).\n   - Groups the remaining national digits in threes separated by spaces, yielding e.g. `+61412345678` \u2192 `+61 412 345 678`.\n\nThe raw `telephone` field is untouched (it's still normalized to E.164 in the controller's `toE164`, unchanged).\n\nThe full test suite passes, including the new `AcceptanceTest.coreFormatsTelephoneDisplay`, which posts `0412 345 678`, then asserts `telephone` = `+61412345678` and `telephoneDisplay` = `+61 412 345 678`.",
